# Git — Branching & Sync (Recipes)

Created: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC  
Last reviewed: 2026-02-02 05:37 UTC

Practical recipes for staying in sync with `main` (or `master`) and keeping branches reviewable.

## Branch Naming
- `ticket/<id>-short-slug` (preferred)
- `fix/<area>-short-slug`
- `chore/<area>-short-slug`

## Start a Branch from `main`
```sh
git fetch origin
git switch main
git pull --ff-only
git switch -c ticket/1234-checkout-spinner
```

## Keep a Branch Updated (Preferred: Rebase)
Use when the branch is not shared or you can coordinate with collaborators.
```sh
git fetch origin
git rebase origin/main
```

If conflicts:
```sh
git status
# resolve conflicts
git add -A
git rebase --continue
```

## Keep a Branch Updated (Alternative: Merge)
Use when rebasing would disrupt collaborators.
```sh
git fetch origin
git merge origin/main
```

## Fast “Clean Up” Before Opening PR
```sh
git status
git diff origin/main...HEAD
git log --oneline --decorate --graph -n 20
```

## Common Pitfalls
- Avoid `git push --force` on shared branches; prefer `--force-with-lease` when you must rewrite history.
- If `git pull` creates a merge unexpectedly, confirm your pull strategy and branch protection expectations.

